<v Speaker 2>The summit is not merely a giver of light like the fire or an incorruptible presence like the ruby.

191
00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:13.550
<v Speaker 2>It is a restorer.

192
00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:15.790
<v Speaker 1>It reaches a soul that has been given up for lost.

193
00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:19.630
<v Speaker 1>The word of the guide serves as the ultimate antidote for souls.

194
00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:27.630
<v Speaker 2>It provides healing from spiritual death, from the illness of ignorance, from paralyzing doubt, and from the vicious discord that fractures human communities.

195
00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:31.070
<v Speaker 1>The soul, dying of doubt, is violently rescued by the guide

196
00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:34.830
<v Speaker 1>just as the dying physical body is rescued by the chemical antidote.

197
00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:47.390
<v Speaker 2>At this critical juncture, seeing the vast scale of this natural hierarchy, seeing the necessity of the rescue, we must restate the core thesis that anchors every single observation we've made.

198
00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:50.270
<v Speaker 2>Every ordered thing has a summit.

199
00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:54.670
<v Speaker 2>The human being is nature's, and the I'm am is humanity's.

200
00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:59.390
<v Speaker 1>With that restorative image established, the author abruptly turns the picture dark.

201
00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:01.390
<v Speaker 1>The argument requires a shadow.

202
00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:08.590
<v Speaker 2>If the true guides are the healing medicines, the potent antidotes, then we must confront the existence of poisons.

203
00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:19.230
<v Speaker 1>The usurpers who seize the place of the true guides by force, who demand obedience without possessing the true light, are likened to the deadly poisons and harmful substances that grow wild in the earth.

204
00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:22.550
<v Speaker 2>The charge against the usurpers isn't a separate isolated attack.

205
00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:24.830
<v Speaker 2>I mean, it rides entirely inside the imagery.

206
00:15:24.830 --> 00:15:28.910
<v Speaker 1>These toxic plants are fatal to whoever listens to them keeps their company or leans toward them.

207
00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:33.950
<v Speaker 1>They destroy the souls of those who trust them unless the true antidote reaches the victim first.

208
00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:36.470
<v Speaker 2>And the usurpers are not just poisons.

209
00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:39.950
<v Speaker 2>The author likens them to useless, worthless stones.

210
00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:41.710
<v Speaker 1>They are the rubble destined for the fire.

211
00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:44.350
<v Speaker 1>They serve no structural purpose in the building of a community.

212
00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:52.670
<v Speaker 2>The text angers this terrifying image in scripture, referencing the pebble-covered hell and the fire whose fuel is people and stones.

213
00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:57.870
<v Speaker 1>The enemies, the usurpers, are gathered into the imagery of worthless rubble and consuming fire.

214
00:15:58.350 --> 00:15:58.790
<v Speaker 1>The single

215
00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:02.910
<v Speaker 1>image of the medicinal plant splits cleanly into two opposing faces.

216
00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:05.710
<v Speaker 2>The cure that saves and the poison that kills.

217
00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:12.750
<v Speaker 1>The author elaborates on this dark mirror by examining the literalists, the AHL as ZAH here.

218
00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:20.510
<v Speaker 2>These are the leaders who cling only to the outer husk of religion, memorizing the rules but entirely missing the healing inner sense of the law.

219
00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:24.910
<v Speaker 1>The text likens them to plants that are bitter, foul, and covered in defensive thorns.

220
00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:29.070
<v Speaker 1>They wound anyone who tries to come near them and they yield absolutely no fruit.

221
00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:32.510
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay relabeling nature as signs.

222
00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:34.590
<v Speaker 2>So it means whatever you need.

223
00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.630
<v Speaker 2>If you want to praise your leader, you call him a sweet fruit.

224
00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:40.910
<v Speaker 2>If you want to attack your political enemy, you call him a bitter thorn.

225
00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:46.990
<v Speaker 2>How is this a binding philosophical argument rather than just convenient poetic name-calling?

226
00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:58.510
<v Speaker 1>The author's rebuttal to that friction is to ground the imagery directly in the ultimate authority of scripture, demonstrating that the Quran itself codes the natural world exactly this way.

227
00:16:58.590 --> 00:16:59.870
<v Speaker 1>It isn't a human invention.

228
00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:08.590
<v Speaker 2>A good word is explicitly described in the text as a good tree with deeply set roots and branches reaching the heavens, giving fruit in every single season.

229
00:17:08.590 --> 00:17:16.830
<v Speaker 1>Conversely, a bad word is described as a bad tree, violently torn up from the surface of the earth, completely lacking any stability, footing, or nourishment.

230
00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:21.270
<v Speaker 2>In this philosophical framework is not a neutral, silent backdrop.

231
00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:24.190
<v Speaker 2>It actively sorts the true guide from the false.

232
00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:29.550
<v Speaker 1>The most immediate of our physical senses, our taste and our smell, are called to the witness stand to testify.

233
00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:37.630
<v Speaker 2>Sweet, delicious, nourishing plants that captivate the human soul and delight the eye represent the speech and the knowledge of the true guides.

234
00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:43.710
<v Speaker 1>The body's natural gravitation towards sweet nourishment is proof of the soul's natural gravitation toward truth.

235
00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:47.510
<v Speaker 2>Meanwhile, the putrid, repellent plants who stench

236
00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:51.550
<v Speaker 2>sickens the stomach rather than heals the body stand for the leaders of error.

237
00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:54.350
<v Speaker 1>What the true summit offers is fragrant and healing.

238
00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:58.190
<v Speaker 1>What the counterfeit offers is bitter, foul, and fatal.

239
00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:05.390
<v Speaker 1>The involuntary physical responses of taste and smell are woven directly into the fabric of the theological argument.

240
00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:15.310
<v Speaker 2>The survey then leaves the rooted plants and moves into the dynamic animal kingdom, where the pattern of summits reasserts itself in its most plain, undeniable, and aggressive form.

241
00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:17.790
<v Speaker 1>We look at the horse, the eagle, and the lion.

242
00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:24.030
<v Speaker 2>Each of these creatures sits at the absolute top of its kind through sheer physical superiority in a specific trait.

243
00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:28.590
<v Speaker 1>The horse is the summit of swiftness, utility, and tireless service in battle.

244
00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:33.950
<v Speaker 2>The eagle holds undisputed, absolute mastery of the air seeing what lesser birds cannot see.

245
00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:37.310
<v Speaker 1>The lion possesses total, terrifying mastery of the hunt.

246
00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:44.670
<v Speaker 1>They prove that every genus naturally, inevitably arranges itself around its highest, most dominant member.

247
00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:50.110
<v Speaker 2>But the author does not stop at creatures that merely dominate their domain through force or speed.

248
00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:53.230
<v Speaker 2>Dominance is not the ultimate peak of nature.

249
00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:55.230
<v Speaker 1>He quietly escalates the argument.

250
00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:58.430
<v Speaker 1>He moves from animals that possess superior traits

251
00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:00.830
<v Speaker 1>to animals that are fundamentally generous.

252
00:19:00.830 --> 00:19:05.310
<v Speaker 2>He looks for creatures that yield a benefit the world could not otherwise possess.

253
00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:09.550
<v Speaker 1>This carries the argument all the way back to the very first criterion established by fire.

254
00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:11.790
<v Speaker 1>The true summit is always a giver.

255
00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:15.630
<v Speaker 2>Among the animals, the gazelle and the bee give the most profound gifts.

256
00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:21.710
<v Speaker 1>The gazelle surrenders its musk, a precious, enduring fragrance used in medicines and perfumes.

257
00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:26.750
<v Speaker 2>And the bee yields its honey, a substance of pure nourishment, preservation, and healing.

258
00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:33.070
<v Speaker 1>The animal survey bends intentionally toward the specific kind of summit the entire treatise is advocating for.

259
00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:36.750
<v Speaker 2>It is not advocating for a lion that merely consumes its prey.

260
00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:39.750
<v Speaker 2>It is advocating for a summit whose excellence overflows

261
00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:42.510
<v Speaker 2>into tangible, life-saving benefit for others.

262
00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:48.110
<v Speaker 1>Within the highly complex structure of the hive, the text finds its most powerful animal emblem.

263
00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:51.390
<v Speaker 1>The author focuses intently on the bee following its chief.

264
00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:53.630
<v Speaker 1>It's Ya-S-O-B.

265
00:19:53.790 --> 00:20:02.830
<v Speaker 2>This is the lead bee, the central authority around which the entire hive meticulously orders its existence, its defense, and its production.

266
00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:06.590
<v Speaker 1>The terminology chosen here is incredibly specific and deeply intentional.

267
00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:13.950
<v Speaker 1>The author points out that the prophet specifically used this exact word when addressing the commander of the faithful, peace be upon him.

268
00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:19.710
<v Speaker 2>He explicitly named him the chief of the believers, using the very term applied to the leader of the hive.

269
00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:24.510
<v Speaker 1>The lead bee of the hive becomes the perfect natural mirror for the father of imams.

270
00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:31.310
<v Speaker 2>The hive orders itself around its chief for one purpose, to produce out of its own body a cure for human beings.

271
00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:37.870
<v Speaker 1>The text cites scripture regarding the bee, noting that from its bellies comes a drink of varying colors containing healing for mankind.

272
00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:48.670
<v Speaker 2>The entire natural survey of potent antidotes, sweet plants, and generous animals circles this single historical figure, a guide whose word operates as absolute medicine for the community.

273
00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:52.190
<v Speaker 1>This brings us to the final sweeping resolution of the latter.

274
00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:57.550
<v Speaker 1>All the creation has been surveyed, from the fire to the ruby, from the palm to the hive.

275
00:20:57.710 --> 00:21:03.550
<v Speaker 2>The text now states plainly that humankind is the ultimate, undisputed summit of all these realms combined.

276
00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:09.870
<v Speaker 1>Everything created before the human being, the raw elements, the precious minerals, the sustaining plants, the animals,

277
00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:13.230
<v Speaker 1>ultimately has its hidden advantage returned to us.

278
00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:19.230
<v Speaker 2>The merits of the entire physical cosmos are gathered up, synthesized, and made to appear in the human being.

279
00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:25.550
<v Speaker 1>The author grounds this grand philosophical claim in the stark daily reality of what human beings actually do.

280
00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:29.230
<v Speaker 2>We do not merely exist passively within nature, hoping to survive it.

281
00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:31.230
<v Speaker 2>We read nature and we command it.

282
00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:32.350
<v Speaker 1>We harness water

283
00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:37.790
<v Speaker 1>drawing it from deep valleys through complex irrigation to force dry land to yield crops.

284
00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:42.190
<v Speaker 2>We strike flint to command fire, turning the most volatile element into our servant.

285
00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:45.310
<v Speaker 1>We crossbreed animals that would never naturally mate in the wild.

286
00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:49.950
<v Speaker 1>We graft branches from different trees to yield fruits the world has never seen before.

287
00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:53.870
<v Speaker 2>We mine deep into the earth to extract jewels from hidden mineral scenes.

288
00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:59.790
<v Speaker 1>Left entirely to themselves, none of these raw materials would ever fully manifest their hidden worth.

289
00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:01.790
<v Speaker 1>The ruby would sit in the dark.

290
00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:04.430
<v Speaker 1>The water would pool in the valley.

291
00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:07.950
<v Speaker 2>The excellence of creation would remain buried and useless.

292
00:22:08.590 --> 00:22:15.790
<v Speaker 2>The human being is the creature at which everything below is perfected, forced into its highest utility and set in order.

293
00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:19.470
<v Speaker 1>Quotes a beautiful piece of poetry to capture this vast enfoldment.

294
00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:27.630
<v Speaker 1>He quotes the father of imams, asking the reader, do you reckon yourself a small thing when within you the greater world lies enfolded?

295
00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:32.990
<v Speaker 2>What that poetry asserts in a single line, the author has spent the entire exhaustive catalog proving.

296
00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:44.030
<v Speaker 2>Domain by domain, he has demonstrated exactly how the greater world, the heat of the fire, the endurance of the stone, the generosity of the bee is gathered into the one creature capable of reading it all.

297
00:22:44.350 --> 00:22:45.910
<v Speaker 1>But here we must push back on the premise.

298
00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:48.750
<v Speaker 1>Are humans truly the summit?

299
00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:52.910
<v Speaker 1>Or are we just another animal near the top of the food chain that happens to have a clever trick?

300
00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:55.550
<v Speaker 2>The horse is significantly faster than a human.

301
00:22:55.870 --> 00:23:00.430
<v Speaker 2>The eagle possesses eyesight that reaches far beyond our biological capabilities.

302
00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:02.350
<v Speaker 2>The lion is vastly stronger.

303
00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:10.350
<v Speaker 1>Why does our ability to manipulate tools and harness water make us a cosmic summit rather than just an unusually intelligent ape?

304
00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:14.910
<v Speaker 2>The counter to that lies in the specific, transformative nature of our reason.

305
00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:21.870
<v Speaker 2>An eagle can see a fish from high above, but an eagle cannot take the raw materials of the earth and build an aqueduct.

306
00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:26.590
<v Speaker 1>A lion can kill a gazelle, but a lion cannot extract the musk to create medicine.

307
00:23:26.590 --> 00:23:32.670
<v Speaker 2>Reason allows the human being to redirect the very course of rivers and force barren ground to bear life.

308
00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:40.710
<v Speaker 1>Without the human intellect to extract, combine, measure, and cultivate, the soil's richness and the seam's buried gem would remain forever latent.

309
00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:46.030
<v Speaker 1>Reason is the precise, unique faculty that makes the hidden worth of creation appear.

310
00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:50.510
<v Speaker 2>But that exact same faculty of reason carries terrifying existential stakes.

311
00:23:50.590 --> 00:24:00.670
<v Speaker 2>Because reason allows us to manipulate and transform our environment, it is also the faculty that, if left ungoverned by a higher guide, becomes an instrument of absolute catastrophic ruin.

312
00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:03.550
<v Speaker 1>This is the hinge of the entire treatise.

313
00:24:03.950 --> 00:24:06.670
<v Speaker 1>This is the crisis the text has been building toward.

314
00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:14.910
<v Speaker 1>Just as the benefit of all lower creation flows upward to humanity, the benefit of all humanity must flow upward to its own summit.

315
00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:19.910
<v Speaker 2>When faced with this logic, you have to concede the terrifying reality of the argument.

316
00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:33.310
<v Speaker 1>I mean, if reason makes us the peak of nature, that very same reason, without the first imam or the guides of any subsequent age to stir the intellect to life and set its measure, makes the human being vastly worse than cattle.

317
00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:38.190
<v Speaker 2>An animal, left entirely to its own devices, does not scheme its own corruption.

318
00:24:38.550 --> 00:24:43.070
<v Speaker 2>An animal has its specific uses and operates safely within its instinctual boundaries.

319
00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:44.990
<v Speaker 1>A lion kills to eat, not to conquer.

320
00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:52.590
<v Speaker 2>But a human being who refuses the training of virtue, who operates solely on brilliant, ungoverned reason, is wholly given over to evil.

321
00:24:52.590 --> 00:24:54.830
<v Speaker 1>The human without a guide is not just a lesser human.

322
00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:59.910
<v Speaker 1>They become the architect of a ruin, a scale of destruction that no beast could ever imagine or execute.

323
00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:03.670
<v Speaker 2>This is why the entire cosmic ladder lands not on a feeling of pleasant

324
00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:06.430
<v Speaker 2>for nature, but on a strict, unavoidable duty.

325
00:25:06.670 --> 00:25:10.030
<v Speaker 1>The cosmos is not an abstraction to be merely observed.

326
00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:11.950
<v Speaker 1>It terminates in an obligation.

327
00:25:12.110 --> 00:25:15.390
<v Speaker 2>Humanity was created to acquire virtues and knowledge.

328
00:25:15.790 --> 00:25:20.030
<v Speaker 2>That acquisition strictly requires receiving direction from the true guides.

329
00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:29.390
<v Speaker 1>If human excellence were never made manifest through this guidance, then the worth of the plants, the animals and the elements would never be truly known either.

330
00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:32.830
<v Speaker 2>There would be absolutely no wisdom in the creation of any of it.

331
00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:36.510
<v Speaker 2>The entire universe would be a pointless exercise.

332
00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:44.990
<v Speaker 1>A Fatimid poet captured this dynamic perfectly, noting that if your tree bears the fruit of knowledge, you will bring the turning heavens beneath your hand.

333
00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:47.390
<v Speaker 2>That fruit ripens from a single root.

334
00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:54.750
<v Speaker 2>The knowledge that elevates a person above the beasts and above the turning stars themselves is received from the guides or it is never received at all.

335
00:25:54.910 --> 00:25:58.830
<v Speaker 1>We've watched the natural witness give its full, unyielding testimony.

336
00:25:59.270 --> 00:26:07.070
<v Speaker 1>From the flame that acts and gives light, to the ruby that endures the crushing pressure of fire and water, to the antidote that restores the dying soul.

337
00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:08.990
<v Speaker 2>The human reality is stark.

338
00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:15.870
<v Speaker 2>The very reason that elevates us to nature's summit can drive us to unparalleled destruction if left unmeasured.

339
00:26:16.030 --> 00:26:19.070
<v Speaker 1>The entire architecture of the physical world demands A pinnacle.

340
00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:21.390
<v Speaker 1>Every ordered thing has a summit.

341
00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.390
<v Speaker 1>The human being is nature's,

342
00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:25.390
<v Speaker 1>and the IMAM is humanities.

343
00:26:25.630 --> 00:26:28.670
<v Speaker 2>Which leaves a final question for you to mull over in your own life.

344
00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:35.630
<v Speaker 1>Which part of your mastery, which part of your own brilliant ungoverned reason currently runs without a guiding measure?

345
00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:38.110
<v Speaker 1>And what changes this week if you seek one?
